The '''1977 Baliscan confederal election''' was held on 9 October 1977 to elect 718 delegates to the [[Confederal Congress]] of [[Balisca]]. The election was the first confederal election since the [[October 76]] crisis and the censure of Secretary General [[Mário Sá Justino]], and resulted in a decisive rejection of the governing [[Revolutionary Front]] (FR) and a landslide victory for anti-authoritarian and ecological platforms.

The Revolutionary Front, which had been a leading platform in Balisca since the 1960s and held 312 seats in the previous Congress, suffered a catastrophic defeat, winning only 36 seats—representing a loss of 276 seats and a collapse from 43.3% to 5.1% of the vote. The [[Popular Assemblies Movement]], an anti-authoritarian coalition formed in response to Justino's emergency measures, emerged as the largest platform with 289 seats. [[Democratic Renewal Platform|Democratic Renewal]] and [[Ecological Action]] also made significant gains, while the [[Indigenous Alliance|Indigenous Sovereignty Alliance]] increased its representation.

The election marked a watershed in Baliscan political history, representing the electorate's rejection of [[W:Authoritarianism|authoritarian]] (specifically [[W:Vanguardism|vanguardism]] and [[W:State socialism|state socialism]]) tendencies and a reassertion of the [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist]] and [[W:Participatory democracy|participatory democratic]] traditions of the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. [[Otávia Brito]], the Popular Assemblies Movement leader, was elected [[Secretary General of Balisca|Secretary General]] in the subsequent [[1977 Baliscan premiership election|premiership election]], becoming the first woman and first openly LGBTQ person to hold the position in Baliscan history.

== Background ==

=== Postponement of the 1976 election ===
Under the Baliscan constitution, confederal elections were scheduled for October 1976. However, in March 1976, Secretary General [[Mário Sá Justino]]—citing escalating tensions with [[Potocsí]] along the disputed border regions and a series of armed skirmishes in the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]—announced the postponement of elections for one year. The decision was made under the provisions of a [[confederal emergency]] declared in February 1976 following the intensification of border clashes and Potocsino military mobilization along the frontier.

The postponement proved deeply controversial. While the Revolutionary Front argued that the security situation required stable governance, critics—including figures within the FR's own coalition—contended that the postponement represented an unconstitutional power grab and a betrayal of democratic principles. [[Confederal Congress|Congress]] had not been consulted on the decision, and several FR delegates publicly broke with Justino over the issue.

=== October 76 protest movement ===

{{main|October 76}}

The postponed election and emergency measures provoked widespread protest. In October 1976, students in [[Castejón]] organized demonstrations against the restrictive emergency measures, including limitations on assembly and increased surveillance powers granted to security forces. The Justino government's response—deploying the [[Guardia Civil (Balisca)|Guardia Civil]] to break up protests—escalated rather than defused tensions.

On 18 October 1976, [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade unions]] declared [[W:Solidarity action|solidarity action]] in support of the students, which quickly expanded into a [[W:General strike|general strike]]. Street clashes erupted between protestors—supported by [[Autonomous Militias]], loyal to local communes—and the Guardia Civil. The conflict split the armed forces, with some units refusing to act against civilian demonstrators while others obeyed orders to suppress the unrest. The crisis severely disrupted Balisca's war effort, as industrial production slowed and military logistics were hampered by the domestic conflict. On 28 December 1976, the [[Confederal Congress]]—including a majority of Revolutionary Front delegates—voted to [[W:Censure|censure]] Justino and ordered the Guardia Civil to stand down. The censure represented an unprecedented rejection of a sitting Secretary General by his own coalition.

